# C. elegans from one cell with terminal fates decided by measured factors.
# The same program as embryo.bio (founder mechanism, the Ma 2021 reader, the observed lineage), plus
# fates.bio (factor rules for intestine, body-wall muscle, sheath glia, hypodermis and neurons). Every
# factor rule carries a priority above the lineage lookup's 0, and the regime takes one fate per decision
# point by priority, so where a factor rule applies to an embryonic terminal cell it decides the fate and
# the observed lookup is only the fallback.
#
# Since 2026-09-15 the rules read `F.exposure(lineage) >= 15`, which the Body integrates itself
# (BIOLANG-v0.4-ECONOMY.md §7.2a) from the factors the reader gives each cell. The generated exposure.bio
# lookup that used to precompute that read is gone: its threshold was a fraction of the largest path
# exposure over the finished run, which no deciding cell can hold.
# Generated rules and scores: scripts/celegans_fate_reads.py, data/results/celegans_fate_reads.json.
#
# What this program's 522 of 555 is and is not (measured 2026-09-21,
# scripts/celegans_fate_credit.py, data/results/celegans_fate_credit.json). The lookup answers every
# cell no factor rule claims and it is Sulston's answer sheet, so 522 is 555 minus the errors the
# factors introduce and not a count of what they know. Credited only where a rule decided:
# 381 of 555 in sample, 374 held out by founder sublineage, and 184 with nothing fitted anywhere
# (the seven textbook rules and the cited sheath-glia rule), against 226 for naming Neuron for every
# cell and 364 for the majority tissue of the founder sublineage. Deeper: every factor rule here is
# guarded on a terminal `cell_type`, which only the lookup ever sets, so no factor rule in this
# program ever decides a fate the lookup has not already written -- delete its 555 fate decisions and
# the rules fire on nothing at all. Open the guard so they can fire, and they claim 105 cells that are
# not terminal against 79 that are, and are right about 44 of the 555. The score is honest; it is just
# not a measurement of the factors, and nothing below this line changes because of the above.
# Run: genomeos grow data/organisms/celegans/embryo_factors.bio --until 800 --compare
module organism.celegans.embryo_factors
# test: cells >= 2150
# test: deaths == 131
import embryo.bio
import fates.bio

regime worm { fates: first }

# A terminal fate, once taken, is not taken again. The rules above read `F.exposure(lineage)`, which
# grows for as long as the cell carries the factor, so a rule whose threshold was not met when the fate
# was settled meets it later for no reason but the clock. Without this lock the worm's score depends on
# how often a cell is asked: 522 of 555 when each cell decides once, 386 with a 6-minute `cell_network`
# cadence re-deciding every live cell. With it, 522 either way, and the shipped program (which declares
# no cadence) is unchanged to the last cell. So it costs nothing here and is what makes the fate a fate.
# Measured: `stability_under_a_re_decision_cadence` in data/results/celegans_fate_reads.json.
commitment terminal_fate {
  establish: cell_type = Coelomocyte|Epithelium|Excretory|Gland|GliaSheath|GliaSocket|Hypodermis|Intestine|Mesoderm|Muscle|Neuron|Other|PharyngealMarginal|Rectal|Reproductive|TailSpike|Valve
  locks: cell_type
  inherit: daughters
  release: never
  evidence: experimental "Fukushige & Krause 2005, Development 132:1795 (the myogenic response is lost completely in terminally differentiated cells, and mes-2 does not restore it)"
  confidence: 0.6
}
